  ### Step 4: Abuse JSON Deserialization Differences (The Trick)

  When parsing JSON:

  • Go's json.Unmarshal matches keys in a case-insensitive manner (e.g. "action" and "Action" both map to the Action struct field). When it encounters
  duplicate/overlapping keys, the last one processed overwrites any previous values.
  • Python's dictionary is strictly case-sensitive. "action" and "Action" are stored as completely different keys.

  If we send this payload:

    {
      "action": "getSecureCode",
      "Action": "getcosmic"
    }

  1. How Go processes it:
      • action is decoded as Action = "getSecureCode"
      • Action is decoded as Action = "getcosmic", overwriting the previous value.
      • Go sees Action == "getcosmic", matches the case "getcosmic" block, and forwards the entire raw payload to Python.
  2. How Python processes it:
      • It decodes the JSON into a dictionary: {"action": "getSecureCode", "Action": "getcosmic"}.
      • When the backend retrieves data['action'] (lowercase), it gets "getSecureCode" and returns the flag.

